    {
      "text": "I already have everything needed from the previous lookup to answer this thoroughly — no additional tool calls required!\n\nHere's a concise overview you can adapt for your course description:\n\n---\n\n**Plot Arc:**\n*To Kill a Mockingbird* is narrated by Jean Louise \"Scout\" Finch, a young girl growing up in the fictional town of Maycomb, Alabama during the 1930s. The novel's central plot follows her father, lawyer Atticus Finch, as he defends Tom Robinson, a Black man falsely accused of raping a white woman named Mayella Ewell. Despite Atticus presenting a clear case for Tom's innocence, the all-white jury convicts him — a stark illustration of the era's entrenched racial injustice. Woven alongside this courtroom drama is a secondary coming-of-age storyline in which Scout, her brother Jem, and their friend Dill become obsessed with their reclusive neighbor Boo Radley, ultimately learning a deeper lesson about empathy and human dignity when Boo saves the children from a vengeful attack.\n\n---\n\n**Primary Themes:**\n- **Racial Injustice** — The trial of Tom Robinson exposes the deep-seated racism of the American South under Jim Crow.\n- **Moral Courage** — Atticus models principled integrity in the face of community hostility and social pressure.\n- **Empathy and Compassion** — A recurring lesson, epitomized in Atticus's instruction to \"climb into someone's skin and walk around in it.\"\n- **Childhood and Loss of Innocence** — Scout and Jem's gradual awakening to adult cruelty and moral complexity drives the novel's emotional arc.\n- **Class and Social Hierarchy** — Maycomb's rigid social strata affect nearly every character's choices and fates.\n- **Justice vs. The Law** — The novel interrogates the gap between legal outcomes and true moral justice.\n\n---\n\nThis should give students a strong orienting framework before the course begins. Let me know if you'd like this tightened further or formatted differently for the registrar's character limits!",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
