      "text": "I have to be straightforward with you again: **my search and page-retrieval tools are consistently returning only generic, surface-level results** for this query — no detailed reception history, no specific contemporary reviews, and no verified censorship records. The tools available in this session are simply not surfacing the granular historical and critical detail you need.\n\nRather than construct a plausible-sounding but unverified narrative (which could introduce errors into your lecture), here is what I can responsibly offer:\n\n---\n\n### What I Can Confirm With Confidence\n\n- **Publication year:** 1960, J.B. Lippincott & Co.\n- **Pulitzer Prize for Fiction:** Awarded 1961 — a strong signal of immediate mainstream critical esteem.\n- The novel became a **bestseller quickly** and was adapted into the acclaimed 1962 film starring Gregory Peck.\n\n---\n\n### Where to Find Reliable Reception History — Recommended Sources\n\nFor your introductory lecture, I'd recommend going directly to these well-established, verifiable sources:\n\n1. **American Library Association (ALA) — Banned & Challenged Books**\nVisit: [ala.org/advocacy/bbooks](https://www.ala.org/advocacy/bbooks)\nThe ALA maintains a comprehensive database of challenged and banned books, and *To Kill a Mockingbird* appears on it regularly. This is the authoritative source for censorship history.\n\n2. **Wikipedia's \"To Kill a Mockingbird\" article — Reception & Legacy sections**\nWhile my tool only returned a summary, the full Wikipedia article contains a well-cited reception history section with references to named contemporary reviewers and later critical reassessments. It's a good secondary starting point with footnotes you can chase.\n\n3. **The New York Times and Atlantic Monthly archives (1960)**\nBoth publications reviewed the novel on publication. Your library's newspaper archive access (ProQuest Historical Newspapers is common) will surface the original 1960 reviews.\n\n4. **\"Fault Lines in *To Kill a Mockingbird*\" and similar retrospective essays** — Searchable via JSTOR or Google Scholar for the evolution of critical opinion, particularly the post-1990s reassessment of Atticus Finch as a problematic \"white savior\" figure.\n\n5. **Isaac Saney, \"The Case Against *To Kill a Mockingbird*,\" *Race & Class* 45.1 (2003)** — This is a well-known critical turning point worth discussing in your lecture, as it represents a significant challenge to the novel's liberal antiracist reputation from a Black critical perspective.\n\n6. **Go Set a Watchman (2015) fallout** — Harper Lee's companion novel, published 2015, famously portrayed an older, segregationist Atticus Finch, and triggered a major public and scholarly reappraisal of the original novel's moral framing. This is a rich area for your lecture's \"evolution of critical opinion\" section.\n\n---\n\nI'm sorry I can't deliver more fully sourced detail directly — I'd rather be honest about my tools' limitations than give you content you can't trust for a lecture. The sources above will get you to exactly what you need. Would you like help structuring the lecture arc itself once you've gathered those materials?",
